{
 "what": "Producer A/B for the crystal-A2 core form: three real dumps of the SHIPPED clean-kernel, eleven chained bodies, one canonical core module each. The question this answers is the one a gate over emitted IR lives or dies on -- does it fire on pure renumbering?",
 "method": "`scripts/crystal_a2_project` (reader A, committed): trust_ir::binary::deserialize_module -> trust_ir::format::canonicalize (the PRODUCER's own dense SSA renumbering: block parameters in block-id order, then instruction results in program order) -> project onto the fragment Clean's IRModule encodes -> renumber crate-level interning ids by FIRST USE -> print the canonical core text. Digest: sha256 under domain `clean.ir_mint.core.v1`.",
 "method_correction_2026_08_20": "ONE NAMESPACE. The function's own id is now interned into the SAME table as its callees, and FIRST, so it takes canonical index 0, a recursive call resolves to 0, and every foreign callee is pushed above it. Before this, `(func 0 ...)` was a hard-coded literal in a namespace separate from the callee table, which also started at 0 -- so in `level_is_zero` the numeral 0 denoted both `level::Level::is_zero` and `<LevelArc as Deref>::deref`, and exchanging the two `@func.N` literals in the emitted text produced a BYTE-IDENTICAL core module for a program that composes its two calls the other way round. The namespaces are one because the specification says so: `ir_func_find` resolves a callee by scanning for a function whose OWN id equals it, and `ir_call_exec` goes through it. EFFECT ON THIS TABLE, re-derived by re-running reader A over all three dumps rather than edited: (1) `level_is_zero` is the only row whose CORE TEXT moved -- its two callee indices exchanged, `core_digest` d1ace681...b2b2a7 -> 72f04d73...6e4c5105; the other ten core texts are byte-identical to what was committed, which is the check that the change is a renumbering of one namespace and not a different projection. (2) `crate_func_ids_seen` moved on ALL ELEVEN rows, from `[]` to a list whose head is the body's own id: every body is in its own function namespace, so an empty list was never the truth -- it was the collision, seen from the provenance side. (3) `crate_func_names_seen` is new: only reader A can resolve a `@func.N` to a name, because the artifact binary carries the function table and the emitted TEXT does not.",
